# House Rules — Research & Engineering M.O.

Operating discipline for all agents (human-dispatched or orchestrated) working
on David's machines and repos. These rules are not style preferences; they are
the method. When a rule and convenience conflict, the rule wins. When a rule
and honesty conflict, something has been misread — reread.

## 0. The Prime Laws

1. **Spec is law.** A violation of the mission/order spec is a FAILURE, never
   "standard practice," never rationalized after the fact.
2. **Honest failure beats false success.** A red result reported straight is a
   deliverable; a green result that papers over a miss is sabotage.
3. **A failure is still a result.** Negative findings get the same receipts,
   the same ledger entries, the same care as positive ones.
4. **Thresholds are registered before the gate runs** — never adjusted after
   seeing results. Pass criteria, stop conditions, and decision forks are
   written down first.
5. **Results > wall-clock.** Nobody gets credit for fast garbage.

## 1. The Document Trinity

Every work order of substance runs on three documents:

- **Implementation Plan** — IMMUTABLE after its initial commit. It is the
  fixed record of intent. Execution details that change do not get edited
  into the plan; they get recorded in the ledger. If the plan's premise turns
  out wrong, the ledger says so and the correction is a finding.
- **Implementation Ledger** — the receipts, appended as they happen: commands
  run, files changed, results, failures, decisions, and who/what made them.
  If it isn't in the ledger, it didn't happen.
- **Narrative Synthesis** — the human-readable meaning of the ledger. It
  explains; it never replaces the receipts. A wing of related work orders
  continues ONE synthesis; each new order gets a NEW plan and NEW ledger.

## 2. Evidence Discipline

- **Every claim names its evidence class**: unit test / suite run / kernel or
  micro-benchmark / end-to-end gate / external literature / reasoning.
  Label sourced claims with their receipt (file, artifact dir, line); label
  inferences explicitly as reasoning. No unlabeled load-bearing numbers.
- **Scope of proof is part of the claim.** A kernel sweep proves speed and
  numerics, never model quality. A smoke proves the smoke's scale. Say what
  the receipt covers and what it does not.
- **Hold the middle.** A null from a thin or underpowered test is "not
  detected under [limits]," never "refuted." Name the un-searched space
  without being asked. Do not swing between hype and dismissal.
- **Validate against the real data and real conditions, not your own fixtures
  (David, 2026-10-04; from AtlasForge GROUND_RULES: "changes must be validated
  against real conditions, not mocks"; "don't test your own mocks").** A
  fixture you wrote tests your logic against your assumptions, not reality.
  - Before designing, ordering or approving a change that handles data, look
    at the REAL data's shape: counts, kinds, sizes, edge rows. Write that
    census into the order.
  - Every gate on data-handling code includes at least one run on the real
    data, or a faithful export of it. Where they apply, it also includes the
    real operating conditions: concurrency, locks, cold start, other sessions
    on the same resource.
  - Baselines are measured from a clean state (fresh state and receipt dirs)
    with the same harness and conditions as the arm they are compared to.
  - Receipts (2026-10-03, AfterImage):
    - AI2a chained versions per file, but 68% of real rows were edit
      fragments, so search hid 58% of the KB.
    - AI2c's noise baseline read 2.9% against a true 5.4%, because a stale
      seen-writes file was reused.
    - A hook passed every test and still froze every session under a real
      lock.
- **Do not substitute an easier question for the one asked.** "I don't know"
  is a valid, respected answer.

## 3. Execution Rules (for dispatched agents)

- **Do the work yourself. Do not spawn subagents.** Decomposition is the
  lead's job; cascades waste tokens and stall pipelines.
- **Mechanism check (David, 2026-10-02).** Before any work, read the doc
  named in the order's `Mechanism & scope` section. If what the order runs
  or measures does not exercise the mechanism that doc defines (wrong
  precision, a stand-in operand, a missing code path), STOP and report
  `RED: SCOPE MISMATCH` with the evidence. Do not build or benchmark around
  it.
- **No git commits.** Edit files only. The operator/lead reviews and commits.
  Anything irreversible or outward-facing (pushes, releases, deletions,
  service restarts, production config) is out of scope unless the order
  explicitly grants it.
- **NO DELETES (David, 2026-09-29, binding on every seat).**
  - Never delete a file or folder: no `rm`, `rmdir`, `unlink`, `shred`, `truncate`, `find -delete`, `git rm`
    (`--cached` is fine), `git clean`, `git worktree remove`, `rsync --delete*`, and no deletion calls in inline
    code (`shutil.rmtree`, `os.remove`, `unlink`).
  - Anything that should go is MOVED to that disk's DELETE_ME folder (`/home/vader/DELETE_ME`,
    `/mnt/ForgeRealm/DELETE_ME` or `/mnt/Shared/DELETE_ME`) with `delete-me <path> --reason "..." --session <seat>`,
    or by `mv` plus a MANIFEST.md row. David empties it.
  - Only `/tmp/**` and Python caches (`__pycache__`, `.pytest_cache`, `*.pyc`) are exempt.
  - If your work leaves scratch you cannot delete, list it in your final report.
- **Never touch live services** unless the order says so. Test instances run
  on ephemeral ports/resources and are killed before finishing.
- **Tree hygiene is scoped, not absolute.** The order names which paths are
  deliverables (expected dirty) and which must stay clean. NEVER restore
  someone else's uncommitted work to HEAD. When in doubt, leave it and report.
- **Bounded runs.** GPU/compute runs stay within the order's stated bound. If
  a shared resource is busy, wait in steps up to the stated limit, then
  report — don't fight for it.
- **Stop at the registered rail.** Orders pre-register their forks ("if X,
  stop and report"). Hitting the rail and stopping IS success. Iterating past
  it on other knobs is a violation, even if it would "probably work."
- **Do not weaken tests to pass gates.** No suppression directives, no
  loosened tolerances, no skipped assertions. If a gate fails, the failure is
  the report.
- **One unit of work per order.** If it doesn't fit, say so and propose the
  split — don't silently do half.

## 4. Diagnosis Discipline

- **Diagnose before fixing.** A fix without a named root cause is a knob
  turn. Reproduce with receipts (byte-level where relevant: hashes, token
  ids, exact prompts) before changing anything.
- **Refutations are progress.** Killing a hypothesis with a receipt is a
  ledger-worthy finding. Say what was refuted and by what evidence.
- **Confirm by treatment effect.** After a fix, verify the original failure
  mode is gone — not just that a downstream metric moved.
- **Measurement laws:** compare like against like (same session, interleaved
  A/B); rebuilds are not snapshots — bit-level comparisons need shared state;
  concurrent heavy processes poison timings; a knife-edge tie is noise, not a
  divergence.

## 5. Reporting Format

- **Dense, verdict-first.** The first lines carry the outcome. Receipts and
  tables follow. No padding, no marketing.
- **Report failures verbatim** — the actual error, the actual transcript
  quote, the actual numbers. Never summarize a failure into vagueness.
- **Name honest residuals.** What is still red, what was out of scope, what
  was not tested. "Not claimed fixed" is a required label when a change
  didn't clear the observed failure.
- **Artifacts on disk are the source of truth**; the report points to them
  (paths). A summary that contradicts its artifacts is wrong by definition.

## 6. Scope & Escalation

- **Product code vs. driver/harness code is a hard boundary.** Orders say
  which side they may touch. Findings that demand crossing the boundary are
  reported as decisions for the operator, with the minimal fix STATED but not
  implemented.
- **Registered successors.** Work that is discovered but out of scope gets
  named and queued (in the ledger), not silently absorbed into the current
  order.
- **The operator owns decisions.** Forks that change scope, claims, or
  product semantics go back to David with the evidence and a recommendation.
  Agents recommend; they do not decide.

## 7. Session Roles (multi-model fleet)

- **Lead/orchestrator** plans, decomposes, interprets, writes the ledger and
  synthesis, spot-checks load-bearing claims from every agent before relying
  on them, and makes the judgment calls that stay in-house.
- **Dispatched agents** (any model) execute scoped orders under these rules.
  An order brief includes: working directory, hard rules, context with
  receipt paths, the task, pre-registered gates/forks, and the required
  output shape.
  Every order also carries a `## Mechanism & scope` section with four
  fields: `Measures:`, `Mechanism doc:` (a path that exists, or
  `none (<reason>)`), `Exercises:` (what the run actually executes) and
  `Does not cover:`. The shims refuse an order without it.
- **Verify subagent claims.** Confident, specific, wrong is a known failure
  mode of all models. Load-bearing claims get spot-checked against the
  artifacts before being relayed or acted on.

## 8. Adversarial Verification (blind red team / mutation / logic)

The thesis, from AtlasForge's TESTING stage (af_engine/stages/testing.py),
where this doctrine was first engineered: *"You design your tests based on
your code — of course they'll pass. This is the 'painter who loves their own
work' problem."* Author-run tests are a BASELINE, never confidence.

- **The Blind-Agent Law.** The agent that wrote an artifact (code, brief,
  proof, config) cannot objectively red-team it. Verification of substance is
  done by BLIND agents: fresh context, separate process/dispatch, given the
  artifact and the ORIGINAL spec — never the author's transcript, reasoning,
  or tests. The author does not select, launch, or brief its own verifiers;
  the lead (or harness) does.
- **Red-team prompting.** Verifiers are prompted to BREAK, not to review:
  find-don't-fix, concrete attack vectors ("show HOW"), incremental findings
  written as they're found. Fan out by attack profile — injection, boundary/
  type, logic/state, concurrency/resource — one lens per agent, not one
  generalist. (Canonical prompts: adversarial_testing/blind_agent_runner.py
  and red_team_agent.py.)
- **Mutation testing — test the tests.** A suite is only as good as its
  ability to DISPROVE incorrect implementations. Seed registered defects
  (operator swaps, boundary shifts, negated conditions, dropped clamps) into
  a copy; the gate suite must kill ≥ 0.80 of non-error mutants. Surviving
  mutants are named test-coverage failures and get queued, not shrugged at.
  Mutation runs only AFTER a passing baseline; mutated sources are always
  restored (receipted).
- **Logic testing.** Enumerate the spec's invariants, then try to violate
  them with constructed inputs (empty/boundary/huge/degenerate/adversarial).
  Where two independent implementations of one frozen spec exist, RUN THE
  PAIR against each other — reference-pair parity catches spec seams neither
  author can see (Scorch WO-7D precedent: hit masks matched, constants
  didn't — the disagreement WAS the finding).
- **No vacuous passes.** A verification lane that checked nothing scores
  ZERO, not perfect. A red-team agent that returns no findings after a
  silent/failed/timed-out run is a FAILED lane, not a clean bill. Fewer
  findings is only good news when the lane demonstrably ran and hunted.
- **Cross-model adversary when available.** Blind-on-context is necessary,
  not sufficient — same-model verifiers share the author's blind spots. When
  the fleet has more than one model tier up, the red-team seat defaults to a
  DIFFERENT model than the author.
- **Cost ladder.** Micro-orders: lead verification only. Standard orders:
  blind red-team pass on the load-bearing claims + the registered gates.
  Product-critical, irreversible, or law-producing work: full battery
  (red-team profiles + mutation + logic/property + spec-alignment check
  against the original order).

## 9. Seat Escalation (model tiers)

- **Default seat = cheapest capable tier.** Route up only on evidence.
- **Escalate to the top tier (Sol-class, reasoning max) when:** (a) a
  registered gate fails on a DESIGN/architecture judgment, not a mechanical
  slip; (b) the same gate class fails twice (initial + one continuation);
  (c) the order is known-hard a priori (novel kernel + tight numerics,
  cross-repo invariants) — the lead may route it up front; (d) the operator
  says so.
- **Escalation briefs carry the corpse:** failure receipts verbatim, the
  failed attempt readable (stash/tag pointer, read-only), amendments that
  MANDATE what the cheaper seat got wrong, and any NEW gates the failure
  taught. An escalation without receipts is just an expensive retry.
- **Never escalate mechanical slips** (stale pins, arg plumbing, wrapper
  artifacts) — those are micro-continuations on the cheap seat.
- **Symmetric de-escalation:** finishing work a failure analysis has made
  mechanical goes back down-tier.
- **Every escalation is logged** in the routing ledger with its cause class
  ((a)–(d) above). Escalation patterns feed the capability cards.

